 \"Viva La Vida\" album. Released three years after Joe Satriani's \"If I Could Fly,\" it faced allegations of sounding too similar, prompting Satriani to file a lawsuit. Coldplay denied wrongdoing and guilt, but an out-of-court settlement was reached and sealed by the court. \"Coldplay released the Viva La Vita album and the title track.\"","year":2008,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Unfortunate Sonic Coincidences","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cZfegexwwk"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"The title track from their 2008 album *Viva La Vida* led to a lawsuit from metal guitarist Joe Satriani, who claimed its melody was too similar to his 2004 song \"If I Could Fly.\" While Coldplay denied wrongdoing and guilt, a \"settlement that was sealed by the court\" was reached. The situation was described as an \"unfortunate Sonic coincidence where Coldplay independently discovered a melody that had already been published and copyrighted by someone else.\" \"Okay, you can see the problem. You compare the melodies of that Coldplay song to If I Could Fly from Joe Centriani.\"","year":2008,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Unfortunate Sonic Coincidences","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rfObVld9rIE"}]}],"book_tracks":[],"others":[{"id":48474469664941,"type":"album","name":"A Rush of Blood to the Head","year":2002,"weight":32},{"id":28715003913540,"type":"album","name":"X&Y","year":2005,"weight":31},{"id":246693311365836,"type":"album","name":"Viva La Vida","year":2008,"weight":19},{"id":1266829771331,"type":"album","name":"Viva la Vida or Death and All His Friends","year":2008,"weight":16},{"id":59733474769163,"type":"album","name":"Parachutes","year":2000,"weight":14},{"id":210148491933880,"type":"song","name":"Talk","year":2005,"weight":13},{"id":103299633778009,"type":"song","name":"Clocks","year":2003,"weight":13},{"id":82635904124717,"type":"album","name":"Everyd